--- !u!114 &2152833601212742420
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 6392382166312832545}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 31629047cadc70941a4f1725fe6382fe, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CelestialBody
  bodyName: Sun
  labelColor: {r: 1, g: 0.98823535, b: 0.36078432, a: 1}
  profileImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 13be78a5b5761794d9a064c0e78b884d, type: 3}
  profileDescription: <b>The Sun is the heart of our Solar System</b>, a massive,
    glowing sphere of hot gas and plasma that provides the light and warmth essential
    for life on Earth. Classified as a G-type main-sequence star (often called a
    yellow dwarf), the Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy, but it holds
    a unique place in our sky and in sustaining the conditions for life as we know
    it.
  stats:
  - label: Mass
    value: <b>1.99 x 10<sup>30</sup> kg</b>
  - label: Diameter
    value: <b>1,392,000 km</b>
  - label: Length of Day
    value: <b>9.9 hours</b>
  - label: Gravity
    value: <b>23.10 m/s<sup>2</sup></b>
  - label: Atmosphere
    value: <b>H2+He</b>
  - label: Temperature (Surface)
    value: "<b>5,000\xB0C</b>"
  orbitCharacteristicsDescription: 
  structureDescription: <b>The Sun's internal structure</b> consists of several layers
    essential to its energy production and Solar System influence. At the core, temperatures
    of 15 million degrees Celsius (27 million degrees Fahrenheit) enable nuclear
    fusion, converting hydrogen into helium and releasing immense energy. \n\nThe
    radiative zone surrounds the core, where energy moves outward through radiation
    over thousands of years.\n\nBeyond it lies the convective zone, where hot plasma
    rises and cooler plasma sinks, transporting energy to the surface.\n\nThe photosphere,
    the visible outer layer, emits the light and heat that reach Earth, while the
    chromosphere and corona extend millions of kilometers into space, reaching over
    a million degrees Celsius.
  atmosphereImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ec57e13ba5da24d409280a178e27a408, type: 3}
  atmosphereDescription: "<b>The Sun's atmosphere</b> is composed of three main layers:
    the photosphere, chromosphere, and corona.\\n\\nThe photosphere, the Sun's visible
    surface, emits the light we see and has a temperature of about 5,500\xB0C (9,932\xB0F).
    Above it lies the chromosphere, a reddish layer visible during solar eclipses,
    where temperatures rise to around 20,000\xB0C (36,000\xB0F). The outermost layer,
    the corona, extends millions of kilometers into space and is incredibly hot,
    reaching temperatures of over 1 million degrees Celsius.\\n\\nThe Sun's atmosphere
    is dynamic, constantly releasing solar wind, a stream of charged particles that
    influences the entire Solar Svstem."
  soundTitle: Parker's Probe Radio Whistlers
  focusDistanceOverride: 0
  minFocusDistanceOverride: 0
  maxFocusDistanceOverride: 30
  galleryEntries:
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 61cbb6c11adc36d4487660b62f21db6b, type: 3}
    title: Sun Emits a Solstice CME
    description: This image from June 20, 2013, at 11:15 p.m. EDT shows the bright
      light of a solar flare on the left side of the sun and an eruption of solar
      material shooting through the sun's atmosphere, called a prominence eruption.
      Shortly thereafter, this same region of the sun sent a coronal mass ejection
      out into space.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 310a25282b885a3479f11782bb07b6eb, type: 3}
    title: Active Star
    description: Flaring, active regions of our sun are highlighted in this image
      combining observations from several telescopes. High-energy X-rays from NASA's
      Nuclear Spectroscopic Telescope Array (NuSTAR) are shown in blue; low-energy
      X-rays from Japan's Hinode spacecraft are green; and extreme ultraviolet light
      from NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory (SDO) is yellow and red.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5e1e973c7a421c74996a4f0031a1a14f, type: 3}
    title: Sun Emits an X2.2 Flare
    description: The sun emitted a significant solar flare, peaking at 12:22 p.m.
      EDT on March 11, 2015. NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory, which watches the
      sun constantly, captured an image of the event. Solar flares are powerful bursts
      of radiation. Harmful radiation from a flare cannot pass through Earth's atmosphere
      to physically affect humans on the ground, however -- when intense enough --
      they can disturb the atmosphere in the layer where GPS and communications signals
      travel.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: a85ff32c03ea6fb488d3909961cf155a, type: 3}
    title: Gigantic Rolling Wave Captured on the Sun
    description: A coronal mass ejection (CME) erupted from just around the edge
      of the sun on May 1, 2013, in a gigantic rolling wave. CMEs can shoot over
      a billion tons of particles into space at over a million miles per hour. This
      CME occurred on the sun's limb and is not headed toward Earth.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 4d5679400a620af4e8bd1f9bd59bf50f, type: 3}
    title: NASA's SDO Sees Giant Filament on the Sun
    description: A dark line snaked across the lower half of the sun on Feb. 10,
      2015, as seen in this image from NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory, or SDO.
      SDO shows colder material as dark and hotter material as light, so the line
      is, in fact, an enormous swatch of colder material hovering in the sun's atmosphere,
      the corona. Stretched out, that line or solar filament as scientists call it
      - would be more than 533,000 miles long. That is longer than 67 Earths lined
      up in a row.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b34343f5651739c408d4b2a437206d31, type: 3}
    title: Magnificent CME Erupts on the Sun
    description: Extremely energetic objects permeate the universe. But close to
      home, the sun produces its own dazzling lightshow, producing the largest explosions
      in our solar system and driving powerful solar storms.. When solar activity
      contorts and realigns the sun's magnetic fields, vast amounts of energy can
      be driven into space. This phenomenon can create a sudden flash of light-a
      solar flare.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 49bb691ba30eec34689898378d80ddae, type: 3}
    title: SDO Spots Extra Energy in the Sun's Corona
    description: These jets, known as spicules, were captured in an SDO image on
      April 25, 2010. Combined with the energy from ripples in the magnetic field,
      they may contain enough energy to power the solar wind that streams from the
      sun toward Earth at 1.5 million miles per hour.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c05476437e726824499c8144056a19be, type: 3}
    title: Sun Shines in High-Energy X-rays
    description: X-rays stream off the sun in this first picture of the sun, overlaid
      on a picture taken by NASA Solar Dynamics Observatory SDO, taken by NASA NUSTAR.
      The field of view covers the west limb of the sun.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 43c00b8b2128edc449dd002d0d8cf1de, type: 3}
    title: Sunspots and Magnetic Fields
    description: 'The physical correlation of magnetic intensity and sunspots is
      clearly revealed when we fade back and forth between a filtered (i.e., white
      light) image of the Sun with a magnetic image (magnetogram) taken the same
      time (Dec. 18, 2014). Two large sunspot groups with strong magnetic intensity
      stand out in both the magnetic and white light images. In magnetogram images
      the stronger black and white areas indicate more powerful polarity. The fading
      between the two kinds of images helps to underscore the magnetic intensity
      of sunspots. These two active regions have unleashed a number of flares. We''re
      keeping a close eye on them or more activity. Credit: Solar Dynamics Observatory,
      NASA.'
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0bc9d9b07a806ac45a21a48207878c0e, type: 3}
    title: Waving Magnetic Loops
    description: 'In profile, the magnetic field lines emerging from several active
      regions were easily observed as they reached across from one magnetic pole
      to another (Sept. 4-5, 2013). When viewed in extreme ultraviolet light, the
      tracings of charged particles along the magnetic field lines are revealed.
      The bright, active regions are areas of intense magnetic forces. This level
      of detail for the entire Sun has never been available before the SDO mission
      became operational. The video clip covers about 18 hours of activity. Credit:
      Solar Dynamics Observatory/NASA.'
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e91c993a583f28341b18dfe4d1a17e66, type: 3}
    title: Sunspot Trio
    description: Over the past two weeks we have been watching since the Active Region
      1504 appeared on the eastern limb of the Sun and formed into a trio of large
      sunspots. They released several M-class (medium) solar flares while moving
      across the Earth facing side of the Sun. As they are moving towards the western
      limb of the Sun, we are getting one good last look at these beautiful sunspots.
      This was captured by NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory.
